    {
      "title": "Why It’s Safer",
      "desc": "Forged disconnects stop working",
      "detail": "With WPA2, anyone in range could forge a “disconnect” frame and knock a device off the network, often to record its handshake when it joined again. WPA3 requires protected management frames, so forged disconnects are ignored. Many routers also offer a transition mode that lets WPA2 devices join too; those devices keep WPA2’s weaknesses.",
      "facts": [
        [
          "Protected",
          "Disconnects, other management"
        ],
        [
          "WPA2/3 mode",
          "WPA2 devices stay at risk"
        ],
        [
          "Stronger still",
          "WPA3-Enterprise (192-bit)"
        ]
      ],
